# Dump the GL client-array state at the play-screen crash, without debug info.
#
#   (lldb) command source /var/jb/var/mobile/rb458-crash.lldb
#
# No frame selection is needed and no frame is used: the renderer is a global, so this reads it
# straight out of memory wherever the process happens to be stopped.
#
# This build has symbols but no DWARF, so `frame variable` finds nothing and `p`/`expr` cannot run
# (the expression checkers will not install on a jailbroken attach). That leaves `memory read` and
# `image lookup`, which is what this uses. There are no persistent ($) variables, so it can be
# sourced more than once in a session.
#
# The offsets below are computed from this build's own member declarations under the AArch64 LP64
# rules, NOT taken from the +0xNN comments in neGLES.h -- those describe the original 32-bit object
# and are wrong here by growing amounts (the element binding is at 0x124, not 0x12c).
#
# CHECK THE LAYOUT FIRST. The last two reads are a self-test: hasMatrixPalette must be 1 and
# maxPaletteMatrices must be 11, which is the value the instrumented build already logged as
# maxPerBatch. If those two do not read 1 and 11, the computed layout is wrong and every other
# number below is meaningless -- say so rather than trusting them.

# If the backtick reads below fail to resolve g_glesRenderer, do it by hand instead: the lookup
# prints the symbol's address ADDR, then
#
#   (lldb) memory read -f x -s 8 -c 1 ADDR      <- gives the renderer pointer PTR
#   (lldb) memory read -f d -s 1 -c 1 PTR+0x238   hasMatrixPalette   expect 1
#   (lldb) memory read -f d -s 4 -c 1 PTR+0x23c   maxPaletteMatrices expect 11
#   (lldb) memory read -f d -s 1 -c 7 PTR+0x1fc   clientStateFlags
#   (lldb) memory read -f d -s 4 -c 1 PTR+0x40    arrayBufferBound
#   (lldb) memory read -f d -s 4 -c 1 PTR+0x124   elementBufferBound
#   (lldb) memory read -f x -s 8 -c 1 PTR+0x48    colourPointer
#   (lldb) memory read -f x -s 8 -c 1 PTR+0x60    vertexPointer
#   (lldb) memory read -f x -s 8 -c 1 PTR+0xf8    weightPointer
#   (lldb) memory read -f x -s 8 -c 1 PTR+0x110   matrixIndexPointer
#   (lldb) memory read -f x -s 8 -c 8 PTR+0x78    texCoordPointer[8]
#
# (substituting the actual hex value of PTR; lldb will not add the offset for you).

# The shared renderer's address. Its symbol holds the pointer; the reads below need its value.
image lookup -r -s g_glesRenderer
memory read -f x -s 8 -c 1 `&g_glesRenderer`

# Layout self-test: expect 1, then 11.
memory read -f d -s 1 -c 1 `*(char **)&g_glesRenderer + 0x238`
memory read -f d -s 4 -c 1 `*(char **)&g_glesRenderer + 0x23c`

# Which client arrays are enabled: 7 bytes, in engine index order
# 0 colour, 1 matrix index, 2 normal, 3 unused, 4 texcoord, 5 vertex, 6 weight.
memory read -f d -s 1 -c 7 `*(char **)&g_glesRenderer + 0x1fc`

# The two buffer bindings the renderer caches.
# arrayBufferBound, then elementBufferBound.
memory read -f d -s 4 -c 1 `*(char **)&g_glesRenderer + 0x40`
memory read -f d -s 4 -c 1 `*(char **)&g_glesRenderer + 0x124`

# Client array pointers. Any of these that is null while its array is enabled above, with no array
# buffer bound, is the fault.
# colour pointer @0x48, vertex pointer @0x60, weight pointer @0xf8, matrix-index pointer @0x110.
memory read -f x -s 8 -c 1 `*(char **)&g_glesRenderer + 0x48`
memory read -f x -s 8 -c 1 `*(char **)&g_glesRenderer + 0x60`
memory read -f x -s 8 -c 1 `*(char **)&g_glesRenderer + 0xf8`
memory read -f x -s 8 -c 1 `*(char **)&g_glesRenderer + 0x110`

# The eight per-unit texture-coordinate pointers @0x78.
memory read -f x -s 8 -c 8 `*(char **)&g_glesRenderer + 0x78`

# Per-array buffer bindings. Each Clear*Pointer re-issues its GL call only when the array buffer
# differs from the value recorded here, so one that disagrees with arrayBufferBound above means that
# array's pointer was never re-established for the buffer currently bound.
# colour @0x54, vertex @0x70, weight @0x108, matrix index @0x120.
memory read -f d -s 4 -c 1 `*(char **)&g_glesRenderer + 0x54`
memory read -f d -s 4 -c 1 `*(char **)&g_glesRenderer + 0x70`
memory read -f d -s 4 -c 1 `*(char **)&g_glesRenderer + 0x108`
memory read -f d -s 4 -c 1 `*(char **)&g_glesRenderer + 0x120`

# The eight per-unit texcoord bindings @0xd8.
memory read -f d -s 4 -c 8 `*(char **)&g_glesRenderer + 0xd8`
